---
description: Mandatory contract for data-analysis scripts in this repository
alwaysApply: true
---

This file overrides any task brief. A PR violating any clause is not merged.

If a clause cannot be satisfied, stop and write `BLOCKED: <one line>` in the PR body. Do not relax a threshold, edit an assertion, or special-case an input.

"CI fails on X" means: the rule is implemented as a check in `scripts/contract_check.py`, which the CI workflow invokes. Add rules to that script; never weaken or delete a rule already in it. The CI workflow file itself, and `.cursor/`, you do not modify.

Do not modify any assertion already present in `tests/`, except in the PR that deletes or rewords the `src/` assertion it matches — list each such pair in the PR body. Counterexample fixtures belong in `tests/fixtures/`, which you may add to freely. `fixtures/schema.sqlite` and `fixtures/manifest.schema.json` change only in a PR whose title begins `schema:` and which touches nothing else; CI on a `schema:` PR runs only the schema-validity and `EXPLAIN` steps.

A file this contract names that does not yet exist is created in the same PR. A missing artifact is never a reason to write `BLOCKED`.

# 1. Checks must be able to fail

1. Every check appends one row to `checks.json`: `{name, status, expected, expected_source, observed, missing}`, `status` in `PASS|FAIL|SKIP`. `expected_source` is `<path>:<key>` naming `frame.yaml`, `rounds/ROUND-N.yaml`, or a file under `expected/` that no script opens for writing. CI rejects the run if the schema is violated, if `expected_source` names a file absent from the commit or written by a script, or if a check reports SKIP on the unmutated fixtures run — except a SKIP whose `missing` names a table or column absent from `fixtures/schema.sqlite`, which passes and is listed in the PR body under `SCHEMA-NEEDED:`.
2. A check that cannot locate the input it claims to read — script, SQL file, column, table — reports SKIP and names the missing thing in `missing`. Never substitute a built-in default, a reimplementation of the rule, or a value recovered by regex from prose. A result derived from a substitute is SKIP: neither PASS nor FAIL.
3. Every assertion carries a message unique in the repository, and a test that trips it with `pytest.raises(<its exception type>, match=r"^<that message>")`. `raise` of any type and `sys.exit("<message>")` count as assertions; an early `return` guarding a data invariant is forbidden and must become a `raise`. CI matches sets, not counts: every assertion message in `src/` must appear as a `^`-anchored `match=` pattern in `tests/`, and every such pattern must resolve to a message in `src/`. The body of a `pytest.raises` must call a function imported from `src/`, unmocked, and may not itself `raise`. A constant, a self-comparison, an algebraic identity, or a file-existence test is not a check: a field such as `smoke_ok` must come from comparing two independently obtained values.
4. `tests/mutations/` holds one patch per defect class whose code path exists in this repository — tier filter removed, stratum weights set to 1, join key swapped, per-stratum seed replaced by the master seed, sentinel written with status `ok`. Each patch touches only `src/`, applies cleanly, and carries a header line `# kills: <assertion message>`. A mutant is killed when the suite fails at that assertion, or when a `checks.json` row that was PASS on the unmutated run becomes FAIL or SKIP; CI fails on a surviving mutant, on a patch for an absent code path, and on an existing code path no patch names. Prose in the PR body is not evidence, and the verdict of a code-review skill is advisory only.

# 2. Missing values

5. Every numeric measure column `c` listed at `frame.yaml: measure_columns` has a companion `c_status` drawn from the enum at `frame.yaml: status_codes`; `unknown` and `other` are not members. CI asserts on every output that `(c_status == "ok")` holds exactly when `c` is non-NULL. `tests/test_null_propagation.py` enumerates its columns from `frame.yaml: measure_columns` and every raw input column named in `sql/*.sql` — never a hand-picked list — nulls one fixture row per column, and asserts every derived value in that row is NULL with `_status` not `ok`. A sentinel number (0, -1, 999, 9999) standing in for a missing value, or any value alongside `c_status == "ok"` not computed from data, fails; `fillna`, `mask`, `where`, `nan_to_num`, `replace`, `COALESCE`, `errors="coerce"`, `or 0`, and `.get(col, default)` are forbidden on a measure column.
6. Any truncation, downsampling, or analysis window occupies an output column (`t0_s`, `t1_s`). Two measurements may share a row or a comparison only when their `(t0_s, t1_s)` are identical for the same `video_id`; CI asserts this on every table declared as a comparison.

# 3. Population, joins, provenance

7. Every run writes `manifest.json`, validated against `fixtures/manifest.schema.json`: per input a sha256, row count and column set, with hashes recomputed and compared in CI; `N_h`, `n_h_sampled`, `n_h_judgeable` and the weight used per stratum; the master seed, every derived per-stratum seed, and `B`; the git sha, `git_dirty`, and the `git hash-object` blob sha of `frame.yaml` as read. It also carries `row_accounting`: one entry per row-count change as `{step, rule, group, rows_before, rows_after}`. A printed number is not a record, and paths alone are not a manifest.
8. `frame.yaml` declares the sampling frame as one filter predicate per line, including the tier whitelist, plus `expected_rows`, `expected_rows_tol` and `expected_rows_source`. `expected_rows_source` names a `sql/*.sql` file counting a raw input table, or a committed count file; it may not read the output of any script here, and `expected_rows_tol` may not exceed 2% of `expected_rows`. CI recomputes `frame.yaml`'s blob sha from the checked-out commit, and fails on mismatch with the manifest or when `git_dirty` is true. The set of rows that happen to join is not a population.
9. Every DataFrame join goes through one helper, `checked_merge(left, right, join_name)`; CI fails on `pd.merge(`, `.merge(`, or `.join(` whose receiver is a DataFrame or Series outside it, and on SQL `JOIN` in a `sql/*.sql` file not listed at `frame.yaml: joins.<join_name>.sql`. The expected row count is a literal at `frame.yaml: joins.<join_name>.expected_rows` — an expected operand referencing a runtime variable is a violation — and unmatched rows are counted into `row_accounting` under that `join_name`.
10. Stratified sampling persists `N_h`. Population-level rates use `w_h = N_h / n_h_judgeable`. Never take an unweighted mean across strata.
11. `frame.yaml: groups.treatment` and `groups.control` are each an explicit predicate. A group defined as the complement of another is a violation. Rows matching neither are counted as `unassigned` in the manifest; a row matching both raises. Inclusion and exclusion rules apply identically to both groups, and rows dropped per rule per group appear in `row_accounting`.
12. Every output table and JSON file except `manifest.json` has a schema under `fixtures/schemas/<name>.json`, validated in CI; `manifest.json` is validated against `fixtures/manifest.schema.json` alone. A writer that would emit a duplicate column or key raises, and a flattened key uses a separator that cannot occur inside a key segment.

# 4. Statistics

13. Report the difference between the group gap and the baseline gap, never the raw group gap. The baseline is the column named at `rounds/ROUND-N.yaml: baseline_model`. Every reported difference lists each group's own value and its baseline value so a reader can recompute it. This governs any row carrying a p-value or interval; a purely descriptive row carries `descriptive=1` and no inferential statistic.
14. Bootstrap by resampling whole `video_id` clusters, with `B >= 1000`. The seed for stratum `h` is `int(sha256(f"{master_seed}:{h}").hexdigest()[:8], 16)`; CI asserts the manifest's per-stratum seeds are pairwise distinct and match that construction. Report `G_h`, the number of videos per stratum, and emit `ci_unreliable=1` where `G_h < 10` — a convention of this project, not a theorem. A row with `ci_unreliable=1`, or with `n_h_judgeable` below `frame.yaml: min_judgeable`, carries `conclusion=inconclusive` and its actual judgeable count. The phrase "not supported" fails CI.
15. Comparisons are declared as a list of ids at `rounds/ROUND-N.yaml: comparisons`. Every output row carries its `comparison_id`; CI asserts `m == len(comparisons)` and one row per id. Report raw and BH-corrected p-values with `m`. A row marked `exploratory=1` carries no p-value and may not be cited in the README or the PR body.
16. A stratifying column must come from a manifest input that is not the output of any script here, and must not appear as a dependent variable in any declared comparison.

# 5. Executability and reproducibility

17. All SQL lives in `sql/*.sql`, loaded only through `load_sql(name)`. CI runs `EXPLAIN` on each file against `fixtures/schema.sqlite` and fails on a parse error, on a `sql/` file referenced neither by a `load_sql` call nor from `frame.yaml`, and — in `src/` outside the module defining `load_sql` — on any string literal containing both `SELECT` and `FROM` as whole uppercase words.
18. Path-bearing arguments and variables carry the suffix `_PATH`, `_DIR`, `_FILE` or `_ROOT` and have no default: CI fails on `environ.get(`/`getenv(` with a second argument, on `environ.get(...) or`, on argparse `default=` for such a name, and on a string literal in `src/` beginning with `/` other than `/dev/null`. A script writing more than one output, or running over 60 s on fixtures, writes to a temporary name and renames, and writes `STATUS.json` as `running` before work and as `complete` with the output sha256 only after the rename; the shared loader refuses any output whose `STATUS.json` is not `complete` or whose sha256 does not match.

# 6. Fixed facts about this corpus

19. The published set is the tier whitelist in `frame.yaml`; any statistic about it filters on tier explicitly and records the rows removed in `row_accounting`. Every recording comes from one YouTube channel, so clustering stops at `video_id`, and the population statement quotes the video counts in `frame.yaml` — never the word "Cantonese".
20. Agreement is `n_match / n_judgeable`, where `n_match` counts syllables whose `jp_match` is `exact_default` or `exact_alt`, and `n_judgeable = n_total - n_empty_realized - n_dur_le_0`. All four counts appear in the output. A NULL `jp_match` on a judgeable row raises.
